    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "thanks. the bricklay thing makes sense. my main concern is when we have pallets that aren't perfectly uniform, like if there's some slight weight shift or if the load isn't perfectly centered. is there a way to predict or compensate for those sudden shifts that might cause a stack to wobble or collapse unexpectedly?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Uniform centers of gravity aren't needed for every single item if you load them tightly against each other to create a rigid, unified mass rather than distinct floating units. For irregular loads, ensure the total weight of any stack is distributed evenly using shear straps or heavy-duty cables to lock the pallets together and prevent individual shifts. If minor instability is still a concern, consider using interlocking base fibers on the ground or heavy-duty dust sheeting systems to add friction and a safety margin against tipping.",
